  "body": "When a file is drag/dropped onto an element, the event passed into the relevant event handlers (for example, the \"ondrop\" handler) is a [DataTransfer](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#datatransfer) object.\n\nThis exposes - amongst other things - an 'items' property, which is a [DataTransferItemList](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#datatransferitemlist), allowing access to the [DataTransferItem](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#datatransferitem)s for that drag/drop operation.\n\nThe 'kind' property on that object is \"string\" or \"file\", but this does not address the case when a user drag/drops a directory into the element.  Firefox simply lies to the JavaScript and puts the directory into the list as a file, which it is not.  Attempts to read that file will fail with a SecurityError.\n\nI propose that the 'kind' property must have the value \"directory\" if the item is a directory has been drag/dropped.\n\nAlternatively, Google Chrome has implemented a different solution whereby DataTransferItem has a \"getAsEntry()\" function on it, returning either a FileEntry or DirectoryEntry object, also indicating whether the item drag/dropped is a directory or not.  This solution could be in the standard instead.  The Chrome implementation is detailed at:\nhttps://developers.google.com/web/updates/2012/07/Drag-and-drop-a-folder-onto-Chrome-now-available?hl=en\n",
